  "query_preview": ".. A closed mid-level low associated with a large mid-\nlevel trough continues to slowly meander east across southeastern \nUT. Ascent will gradually increase along its eastern periphery into \nthe afternoon, bringing another round of low elevation rainfall and \nsnowfall to the western Uintas and central UT mountains into \ntomorrow morning. \n\nAs the low continues east, a lobe of vorticity will dip south \ntowards the AZ/CA border spinning up a large, albeit weak, trough \nacross much of the southwest. This feature will meander across the \narea into the weekend, slowly retrogressing southwest and weakening \nwith time as westerly flow develops across the Great Basin in its \nwake. This will yield mild conditions with a slight uptick in \nafternoon temperatures through the weekend with a dry pattern \ndeveloping. Eventually, this trough will weaken enough to phase back \ninto weak mean mid-level flow by Monday ahead of an incoming system.\n\nCurrent model trends suggest a more east-northeast mid-level wind \ncomponent across northern UT this evening into tomorrow morning, \nsuggesting the canyon wind / localized downslope event will be more \nlimited than anticipated. This is due to the mid-level low sliding \nsomewhat further northeast over time than initially forecast in \nprior cycles. Regardless, typically windy spots (such as Ogden and \nFarmington canyons) may see gusts to 45mph in the immediate \nvicinities of each respective canyon mouth. \n\nAcross southwest UT, a gap wind event still appears likely to \ndevelop late this evening and persist into tomorrow morning as \nnorthwest flow amidst a tight pressure gradient continues to \nestablish itself. While gusts will generally range from 30-40mph \nthrough much of the overnight period, gusts potentially reaching or \nexceeding 45mph (70% chance) look to develop around the 2-6AM \ntimeframe primarily near the Beaver Dam Mountains and Blackrock \nCanyon. Winds are expected to diminish across the area before noon.\n\nAnother Pacific system looks to push into the western U.S. around \nTuesday with favorable agreement among ensemble members (>80%). \nCompared to yesterday, however, ensembles are now split regarding \ntwo solutions which would greatly impact what we see across the \nforecast area. The first solution keeps the mid-level trough in \ntact, bringing widespread precipitation and mountain snow to UT and \nsouthwest WY. The mid-level trough remaining in tact is particularly \nimportant to this scenario as it will draw in notably colder air, \nresulting in favorable mountain snow conditions for much of the \nstate. The latter solution is, unfortunately, quite similar to what \nwe saw many systems do at the start of the 2025-2026 season where \nthe system \"cuts off\" and forms a cutoff low to the south of the \nSierra mountains. This will prevent more robust cold air advection \nfurther south, resulting in a warmer system favoring snowfall across \nhigher elevation southern and central UT terrain. Additionally, this \nwould result in weaker ascent further north with northern UT seeing \nweaker rain showers and little snow across the terrain. These early \nseason systems are typically difficult to resolve at this timeframe, \nand this forecast will more than likely see major changes over the \ncoming days.",
